1
00:00:00,472 --> 00:00:00,854
Sir.

2
00:00:00,894 --> 00:00:02,798
A new field report from planet Earth.

3
00:00:02,838 --> 00:00:03,645
Today's subject:

4
00:00:03,685 --> 00:00:04,664
cryptocurrency.

5
00:00:04,704 --> 00:00:06,453
Money the humans invented without a bank.

6
00:00:06,493 --> 00:00:07,492
And without paper.

7
00:00:07,532 --> 00:00:08,928
I will tell you what it is, where

8
00:00:08,968 --> 00:00:10,408
it came from, and who made it.

9
00:00:10,448 --> 00:00:11,754
And how one of them paid for two

10
00:00:11,794 --> 00:00:13,548
pizzas with what is now worth hundreds of

11
00:00:13,588 --> 00:00:14,656
millions of dollars.

12
00:00:14,696 --> 00:00:15,664
The year 2010.

13
00:00:15,704 --> 00:00:16,928
Ten thousand bitcoin...

14
00:00:16,968 --> 00:00:18,371
for two pizzas.

15
00:00:18,468 --> 00:00:19,810
At the prices of recent years, that comes

16
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:21,352
to hundreds of millions of dollars.

17
00:00:21,392 --> 00:00:22,852
The most expensive dinner on the planet.

18
00:00:22,892 --> 00:00:24,182
And not even deep dish.

19
00:00:24,222 --> 00:00:25,883
But for Command to understand any of this,

20
00:00:25,923 --> 00:00:26,811
I have to start with the first thing

21
00:00:26,851 --> 00:00:27,584
I studied here:

22
00:00:27,624 --> 00:00:28,423
money itself.

23
00:00:28,463 --> 00:00:29,518
I checked their history.

24
00:00:29,558 --> 00:00:31,011
Long ago they swapped things.

25
00:00:31,051 --> 00:00:32,177
A fish for a loaf of bread.

26
00:00:32,217 --> 00:00:32,796
The problem:

27
00:00:32,836 --> 00:00:34,419
if the baker wants no fish today, you

28
00:00:34,459 --> 00:00:34,922
go hungry.

29
00:00:34,962 --> 00:00:35,601
Holding a fish.

30
00:00:35,641 --> 00:00:37,483
So they tried other things everybody would take.

31
00:00:37,523 --> 00:00:37,999
Salt.

32
00:00:38,039 --> 00:00:38,557
Shells.

33
00:00:38,597 --> 00:00:40,190
And on one island, stones as big as

34
00:00:40,230 --> 00:00:40,723
a car.

35
00:00:40,763 --> 00:00:41,930
Imagine asking for change.

36
00:00:41,970 --> 00:00:43,293
They settled on gold:

37
00:00:43,333 --> 00:00:45,470
rare, never rots, hard to fake.

38
00:00:46,283 --> 00:00:47,363
But gold is heavy.

39
00:00:47,403 --> 00:00:49,185
So they left it with someone trusted and

40
00:00:49,225 --> 00:00:49,941
took a receipt.

41
00:00:49,981 --> 00:00:51,390
The receipt started changing hands:

42
00:00:51,430 --> 00:00:52,135
paper money.

43
00:00:52,175 --> 00:00:54,079
Then the trusted man discovered he could lend

44
00:00:54,119 --> 00:00:55,493
your gold to someone else while you were

45
00:00:55,533 --> 00:00:56,111
not looking.

46
00:00:56,151 --> 00:00:57,437
And that, sir, is how the bank was

47
00:00:57,477 --> 00:00:57,913
born.

48
00:00:57,953 --> 00:01:00,307
In 1971 the dollar was cut loose from

49
00:01:00,347 --> 00:01:00,825
gold.

50
00:01:00,865 --> 00:01:01,995
The paper stood on its own.

51
00:01:02,035 --> 00:01:03,882
And whoever runs the printing press can print

52
00:01:03,922 --> 00:01:05,130
as much as they like.

53
00:01:05,170 --> 00:01:07,069
In Zimbabwe, they printed a single note for

54
00:01:07,109 --> 00:01:08,727
one hundred trillion dollars.

55
00:01:08,767 --> 00:01:10,250
It would not buy a bus ticket.

56
00:01:10,290 --> 00:01:12,288
So money's value comes from one thing:

57
00:01:12,328 --> 00:01:13,676
everyone believes in it.

58
00:01:13,716 --> 00:01:14,642
A group agreement.

59
00:01:14,682 --> 00:01:16,001
Like a line at the coffee shop.

60
00:01:16,041 --> 00:01:17,619
It works until somebody cuts.

61
00:01:17,659 --> 00:01:19,121
And today most of their money is not

62
00:01:19,161 --> 00:01:19,819
even paper.

63
00:01:19,859 --> 00:01:21,166
It is numbers on a screen.

64
00:01:21,206 --> 00:01:22,796
Most money on this planet has no physical

65
00:01:22,836 --> 00:01:23,228
form.

66
00:01:23,268 --> 00:01:24,189
Entries in tables.

67
00:01:24,229 --> 00:01:25,543
And they are perfectly comfortable with that.

68
00:01:25,583 --> 00:01:27,269
I also recorded that their money has three

69
00:01:27,309 --> 00:01:27,940
jobs.

70
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:28,647
You pay with it.

71
00:01:28,687 --> 00:01:29,747
You price things with it.

72
00:01:29,787 --> 00:01:31,020
And you save it for tomorrow.

73
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:32,210
Keep those three in the file, sir.

74
00:01:32,250 --> 00:01:33,021
We will need them later.

75
00:01:33,061 --> 00:01:35,104
I traced those numbers to where they actually

76
00:01:35,144 --> 00:01:35,670
live:

77
00:01:35,710 --> 00:01:36,484
in a ledger.

78
00:01:36,524 --> 00:01:38,460
Kept by an entity called the bank.

79
00:01:38,551 --> 00:01:41,314
When Alice pays Bob thirty dollars, nothing moves.

80
00:01:41,354 --> 00:01:43,511
The bank erases a number here, writes one

81
00:01:43,551 --> 00:01:44,008
there.

82
00:01:44,048 --> 00:01:44,676
Your salary:

83
00:01:44,716 --> 00:01:45,161
a number.

84
00:01:45,201 --> 00:01:45,745
Your rent:

85
00:01:45,785 --> 00:01:46,254
a number.

86
00:01:46,294 --> 00:01:48,325
You have never actually seen this money.

87
00:01:48,365 --> 00:01:49,849
Every bank keeps its own ledger.

88
00:01:49,889 --> 00:01:51,601
So every night they check each other's.

89
00:01:51,641 --> 00:01:53,579
Because none of them trusts the others.

90
00:01:53,619 --> 00:01:55,151
So a bank is not a vault full

91
00:01:55,191 --> 00:01:55,780
of money.

92
00:01:55,820 --> 00:01:57,017
A bank is a ledger.

93
00:01:57,057 --> 00:01:59,351
And whoever holds the ledger holds everything.

94
00:01:59,432 --> 00:02:01,433
I followed one transfer from start to finish.

95
00:02:01,473 --> 00:02:03,366
Every cent that moves between two humans has

96
00:02:03,406 --> 00:02:04,803
to pass through a third party.

97
00:02:04,843 --> 00:02:05,655
Alice sends.

98
00:02:05,695 --> 00:02:06,509
The bank checks.

99
00:02:06,549 --> 00:02:07,474
Then Bob receives it.

100
00:02:07,514 --> 00:02:08,304
Minus the fee.

101
00:02:08,344 --> 00:02:10,313
The ledger holder can charge a fee, delay

102
00:02:10,353 --> 00:02:12,596
you, freeze your account, or go bankrupt with

103
00:02:12,636 --> 00:02:13,145
your money.

104
00:02:13,185 --> 00:02:14,239
This is not a theory.

105
00:02:14,279 --> 00:02:16,371
On the island of Cyprus, in 2013, people

106
00:02:16,411 --> 00:02:18,224
woke up to find the banks closed.

107
00:02:18,264 --> 00:02:19,693
Anyone with over a hundred thousand euros in

108
00:02:19,733 --> 00:02:21,881
the biggest bank lost almost half the excess.

109
00:02:21,921 --> 00:02:23,115
To rescue the bank.

110
00:02:23,155 --> 00:02:25,224
In Lebanon, since 2019, banks have locked up

111
00:02:25,264 --> 00:02:26,096
people's dollars.

112
00:02:26,136 --> 00:02:27,578
Years later, they are still asking.

113
00:02:27,618 --> 00:02:29,672
Their messages cross the planet in a second.

114
00:02:29,712 --> 00:02:31,465
A bank transfer can take three days.

115
00:02:31,505 --> 00:02:33,063
Both are just numbers, sir.

116
00:02:33,103 --> 00:02:34,604
And over a billion adults here have no

117
00:02:34,644 --> 00:02:35,161
bank account.

118
00:02:35,201 --> 00:02:36,308
No line in the ledger.

119
00:02:36,348 --> 00:02:37,592
As far as the system is concerned, they

120
00:02:37,632 --> 00:02:38,910
do not exist.

121
00:02:38,998 --> 00:02:41,004
So I asked the obvious question:

122
00:02:41,044 --> 00:02:43,197
why not digital money without a bank?

123
00:02:43,237 --> 00:02:43,809
A file.

124
00:02:43,849 --> 00:02:45,012
You send it, done.

125
00:02:45,112 --> 00:02:46,627
The problem is that anything digital can be

126
00:02:46,667 --> 00:02:47,259
copied.

127
00:02:47,299 --> 00:02:49,146
A photo, a song, a movie.

128
00:02:49,186 --> 00:02:50,089
Copy, paste.

129
00:02:50,129 --> 00:02:50,890
Free.

130
00:02:50,930 --> 00:02:52,408
If money were a file, Alice could send

131
00:02:52,448 --> 00:02:54,517
the same hundred dollars to Bob, and to

132
00:02:54,557 --> 00:02:54,972
Carol.

133
00:02:55,012 --> 00:02:55,534
At once.

134
00:02:55,574 --> 00:02:57,564
That is called the double-spending problem.

135
00:02:57,604 --> 00:02:58,753
And that is why they needed someone in

136
00:02:58,793 --> 00:03:00,909
the middle, keeping track of who paid what.

137
00:03:00,949 --> 00:03:02,683
In the nineties, a group of programmers who

138
00:03:02,723 --> 00:03:05,256
called themselves the Cypherpunks dreamed of private digital

139
00:03:05,296 --> 00:03:05,788
money.

140
00:03:05,828 --> 00:03:06,586
Nobody watching it.

141
00:03:06,626 --> 00:03:07,682
Nobody able to stop it.

142
00:03:07,722 --> 00:03:09,307
Humans had been trying since the eighties.

143
00:03:09,347 --> 00:03:11,058
A scientist named David Chaum started a digital

144
00:03:11,098 --> 00:03:11,825
cash company.

145
00:03:11,865 --> 00:03:13,267
It went bankrupt in 1998.

146
00:03:13,307 --> 00:03:14,970
The digital money ran out of money.

147
00:03:15,010 --> 00:03:17,286
And then came the thing that changed everything:

148
00:03:17,326 --> 00:03:18,437
2008.

149
00:03:18,477 --> 00:03:20,941
Big American banks had bet people's money on

150
00:03:20,981 --> 00:03:21,984
home loans...

151
00:03:22,024 --> 00:03:22,913
and lost.

152
00:03:22,953 --> 00:03:24,686
A bank called Lehman Brothers, more than a

153
00:03:24,726 --> 00:03:26,877
hundred and fifty years old, went under in

154
00:03:26,917 --> 00:03:27,838
a single day.

155
00:03:27,878 --> 00:03:30,668
The American government set aside seven hundred billion

156
00:03:30,708 --> 00:03:32,408
dollars to rescue the banks.

157
00:03:32,448 --> 00:03:33,456
With whose money?

158
00:03:33,496 --> 00:03:34,585
The same people's.

159
00:03:34,886 --> 00:03:36,772
Millions lost their homes and jobs.

160
00:03:36,812 --> 00:03:37,922
And the rescued banks?

161
00:03:37,962 --> 00:03:39,979
Nine of them paid over thirty billion dollars

162
00:03:40,019 --> 00:03:41,566
in bonuses that same year.

163
00:03:41,606 --> 00:03:43,500
So the ledger keeper gambled, and the customer

164
00:03:43,540 --> 00:03:43,894
paid.

165
00:03:43,934 --> 00:03:44,822
Heads, the bank wins.

166
00:03:44,862 --> 00:03:45,773
Tails, you pay.

167
00:03:45,813 --> 00:03:46,469
Brilliant, sir.

168
00:03:46,509 --> 00:03:47,358
I recommend we adopt it.

169
00:03:47,398 --> 00:03:49,106
Right in the middle of that crisis, a

170
00:03:49,146 --> 00:03:51,728
nine-page paper appeared, sent to a mailing list

171
00:03:51,768 --> 00:03:53,501
of people interested in cryptography.

172
00:03:53,541 --> 00:03:53,912
The date:

173
00:03:53,952 --> 00:03:55,985
October 31st, 2008.

174
00:03:56,025 --> 00:03:56,884
Halloween.

175
00:03:56,924 --> 00:03:59,042
A masked stranger handing out something free.

176
00:03:59,082 --> 00:04:00,530
Their children are warned about this.

177
00:04:00,617 --> 00:04:01,095
The title:

178
00:04:01,135 --> 00:04:03,937
Bitcoin, a peer-to-peer electronic cash system.

179
00:04:03,996 --> 00:04:04,652
One promise:

180
00:04:04,692 --> 00:04:07,261
send money to anyone, directly, asking nobody's permission.

181
00:04:07,301 --> 00:04:07,835
The author:

182
00:04:07,875 --> 00:04:09,114
Satoshi Nakamoto.

183
00:04:09,154 --> 00:04:10,557
To this day, nobody knows who that is.

184
00:04:10,597 --> 00:04:12,159
A man, a woman, or a group.

185
00:04:12,211 --> 00:04:13,384
And before anyone asks, sir:

186
00:04:13,424 --> 00:04:14,764
no, it was not me.

187
00:04:14,804 --> 00:04:15,620
I arrived later.

188
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:17,496
The idea was simple and bold:

189
00:04:17,536 --> 00:04:19,117
take the ledger away from the bank.

190
00:04:19,157 --> 00:04:21,111
Every computer on the network gets a full

191
00:04:21,151 --> 00:04:22,177
copy of the ledger.

192
00:04:22,217 --> 00:04:24,344
Thousands of computers, all around the planet.

193
00:04:24,384 --> 00:04:26,441
When Alice pays Bob, the news reaches them

194
00:04:26,481 --> 00:04:28,899
all, and every computer writes the same line.

195
00:04:28,939 --> 00:04:30,420
And if someone tries to cheat by editing

196
00:04:30,460 --> 00:04:32,120
their own copy, everyone else says:

197
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:32,523
no.

198
00:04:32,563 --> 00:04:33,502
That is not what we have.

199
00:04:33,542 --> 00:04:34,834
The ledger is also public.

200
00:04:34,874 --> 00:04:36,367
Anyone can read it all, from the first

201
00:04:36,407 --> 00:04:36,750
line.

202
00:04:36,790 --> 00:04:38,341
But the names are not names.

203
00:04:38,381 --> 00:04:40,391
They are long strings of letters and numbers.

204
00:04:40,431 --> 00:04:42,374
Everyone sees everything, and nobody knows who is

205
00:04:42,414 --> 00:04:42,777
who.

206
00:04:42,817 --> 00:04:44,561
A masked ball, with receipts.

207
00:04:44,601 --> 00:04:45,325
No manager.

208
00:04:45,365 --> 00:04:46,123
No head office.

209
00:04:46,163 --> 00:04:47,567
And no customer service line keeping you on

210
00:04:47,607 --> 00:04:49,168
hold for forty minutes with smooth jazz.

211
00:04:49,208 --> 00:04:51,265
Which also means nobody can freeze your account,

212
00:04:51,305 --> 00:04:53,717
or print extra bitcoin to cover a deficit.

213
00:04:53,757 --> 00:04:55,499
The rules are written in code, and the

214
00:04:55,539 --> 00:04:56,942
code is out in the open.

215
00:04:57,142 --> 00:04:59,078
This shared ledger is called the blockchain.

216
00:04:59,118 --> 00:05:00,852
And how thousands of strangers agree on one

217
00:05:00,892 --> 00:05:02,262
copy without trusting each other?

218
00:05:02,302 --> 00:05:03,860
That is the subject of the next report.

219
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:05,695
January 3rd, 2009.

220
00:05:05,735 --> 00:05:07,854
Satoshi switched the network on, and wrote the

221
00:05:07,894 --> 00:05:09,228
first page of the ledger:

222
00:05:09,268 --> 00:05:10,288
block number zero.

223
00:05:10,328 --> 00:05:12,632
And inside that block, Satoshi left a message.

224
00:05:12,672 --> 00:05:14,474
The front-page headline of The Times of London

225
00:05:14,514 --> 00:05:14,943
that day:

226
00:05:14,983 --> 00:05:17,151
Chancellor on brink of second bailout for banks.

227
00:05:17,191 --> 00:05:18,815
So the very first line of money without

228
00:05:18,855 --> 00:05:19,462
banks...

229
00:05:19,502 --> 00:05:20,913
is a dig at the banks.

230
00:05:20,953 --> 00:05:22,493
This creature had a sense of humor, sir.

231
00:05:22,533 --> 00:05:25,163
That block also created fifty brand new bitcoin.

232
00:05:25,203 --> 00:05:25,890
Out of thin air.

233
00:05:25,930 --> 00:05:27,823
But under rules written down from day one,

234
00:05:27,863 --> 00:05:29,572
which no single person can change.

235
00:05:29,612 --> 00:05:31,045
Quite different from the printing they are used

236
00:05:31,085 --> 00:05:31,362
to.

237
00:05:31,402 --> 00:05:33,167
Nine days later came the first transfer:

238
00:05:33,207 --> 00:05:35,555
ten bitcoin, to a programmer named Hal Finney.

239
00:05:35,595 --> 00:05:37,204
The first two people on the network.

240
00:05:37,244 --> 00:05:38,495
One of them anonymous.

241
00:05:38,535 --> 00:05:39,903
At first, nobody cared.

242
00:05:39,943 --> 00:05:41,519
Bitcoin was practically given away.

243
00:05:41,559 --> 00:05:42,844
The first exchange rate on record:

244
00:05:42,884 --> 00:05:44,417
more than a thousand bitcoin...

245
00:05:44,457 --> 00:05:45,622
for one dollar.

246
00:05:45,662 --> 00:05:46,830
Until May 2010.

247
00:05:46,870 --> 00:05:49,035
A programmer named Laszlo posted online:

248
00:05:49,075 --> 00:05:50,887
ten thousand bitcoin to whoever gets me two

249
00:05:50,927 --> 00:05:51,510
pizzas.

250
00:05:51,550 --> 00:05:52,843
And on May 22nd...

251
00:05:52,883 --> 00:05:53,658
somebody did.

252
00:05:53,698 --> 00:05:55,545
It was the first time bitcoin bought something

253
00:05:55,585 --> 00:05:56,343
real.

254
00:05:56,383 --> 00:05:58,723
Laszlo later said he has no regrets.

255
00:05:58,763 --> 00:05:59,756
The pizza was good.

256
00:05:59,796 --> 00:06:01,225
And the humans still celebrate that day every

257
00:06:01,265 --> 00:06:01,767
year.

258
00:06:01,807 --> 00:06:03,356
They call it Bitcoin Pizza Day.

259
00:06:03,477 --> 00:06:04,519
They hold a party for the worst deal

260
00:06:04,559 --> 00:06:05,208
in their history.

261
00:06:05,248 --> 00:06:06,735
I am starting to like this planet.

262
00:06:06,811 --> 00:06:08,436
Less than a year later, one bitcoin reached

263
00:06:08,476 --> 00:06:09,308
one dollar.

264
00:06:09,348 --> 00:06:10,101
A whole dollar.

265
00:06:10,141 --> 00:06:11,554
And the humans thought that was the top.

266
00:06:11,594 --> 00:06:12,862
As for Satoshi:

267
00:06:12,902 --> 00:06:15,747
in April 2011 came the last known message.

268
00:06:15,787 --> 00:06:17,425
I've moved on to other things.

269
00:06:17,465 --> 00:06:18,096
Then, gone.

270
00:06:18,136 --> 00:06:20,398
First, Satoshi handed the code to other programmers.

271
00:06:20,438 --> 00:06:21,165
No farewell.

272
00:06:21,205 --> 00:06:22,188
No press conference.

273
00:06:22,228 --> 00:06:22,920
Not even a photo.

274
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:25,005
And left behind about a million bitcoin, by

275
00:06:25,045 --> 00:06:25,981
researchers' estimates.

276
00:06:26,021 --> 00:06:27,399
As far as the humans can tell, those

277
00:06:27,439 --> 00:06:28,873
coins have never moved.

278
00:06:28,913 --> 00:06:30,375
Invented a new money, became one of the

279
00:06:30,415 --> 00:06:32,255
richest beings on the planet, and never spent

280
00:06:32,295 --> 00:06:32,809
a cent.

281
00:06:32,849 --> 00:06:34,049
Not human behavior, sir.

282
00:06:34,089 --> 00:06:35,235
I request an investigation.

283
00:06:35,275 --> 00:06:36,624
Satoshi may be one of ours.

284
00:06:36,664 --> 00:06:38,543
